# Pinned package-repo snapshot — Valve's official Steam Frame ("deckard") aarch64 Arch Linux port.
#
# Every snapshot/stripped row of rootfs/manifest.lock and installer/manifest.lock is installed from
# this repo, and build/builder.pin pins the SAME snapshot's system.rootfs.zst as the builder, so the
# pacman laying a root down comes from the snapshot it lays down.
#
# Tree: https://holo-packages.steamos.cloud/archlinux-deckard/archlinux/<snapshot>/<repo>/os/aarch64
# Each snapshot publishes core, extra (+ -debug), extra-archive, multilib, sources/, its own
# pacman.conf (only [core] and [extra], SigLevel = Optional — no detached signatures anywhere), and
# system.rootfs.zst. We enable the same two repos (rootfs/conf/pacman.conf).
#
# ALIASES — the directory names are not all frozen snapshots:
#   main, dev, builds, pipeline, tmp   moving CI aliases. Never pinned.
#   mash-YYYYMMDD                      an ALIAS that follows the newest revision of its date
#                                      (mash-20260305 moved .1.pvt -> .2.pvt on 2026-10-13).
#                                      Refused: a pin on it moves under us.
#   mash-YYYYMMDD.N                    a frozen revision.
#   mash-YYYYMMDD.N.pvt                a frozen revision on the second publishing line — the one the
#                                      tree actually publishes for 2026 dates (no plain .N exists).
# Only a frozen revision passes build/lib-pins.sh. The per-package sha256 in the locks stays the
# real pin regardless: rootfs/fetchlock.sh verifies every file, so a republish fails the build.
#
# Pin = last non-comment, non-blank line: a repo base URL. build/lib-pins.sh validates it (all
# stages source it) and the stages append /$repo/os/$arch.
# Bumping is deliberate: change this line AND build/builder.pin, `make relock`, review both locks.
# .github/workflows/upstream-watch.yml (deckard-base) opens an issue when a newer date OR a newer
# revision of the pinned date is published.
#
# The public mash is NOT everything the Steam Frame ships: Valve layers a private holo repo on top.
# mash-20260401 dropped Arch's podman stack while the Frame still installs Valve-CI builds of it, so
# a package leaving this tree is not Valve retiring it — packages/ now builds that stack itself.
https://holo-packages.steamos.cloud/archlinux-deckard/archlinux/mash-20260501.1.pvt
